<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    Plugins ▸ Matter, in five sections (issue #134).

    SEPARATORS: an EMPTY, self-closing <MenuItem id="…"/> renders as a menu
    separator. This is undocumented — the canonical MenuItems.xml reference
    lists <Name> as required and describes neither separators nor submenus —
    but it is what Perceptive Automation's own bundled plugins do (Timers and
    Pesters, Timed Devices, Virtual Devices, Alexa), and Actions.xml uses the
    same trick (<Action id="sep1" uiPath="…"/>). Fifteen plugins installed on
    jarvis rely on it. Do not "fix" these into named items.

    The ordering is the whole point, so tests/test_plugin_module.py asserts the
    ORDER and the separator positions, not just that each item exists.
    (tests/test_export_menu.py owns the Manage Matter Exports dialog's shape.)

    The five sections, everyday work first and the destructive things fenced
    off last:

      1. Matter devices Indigo CONTROLS (inbound)  — the everyday actions
      2. Matter devices Indigo PUBLISHES (export)  — the everyday actions
      3. The Matter controller (matter-server)     — install/restart/logs
      4. The Matter bridge node                    — install/restart/stop
      5. Backup and recovery                       — rare, and some destructive

    Sections 3 and 4 exist as separate blocks because their Install/update
    items used to sit eleven apart and were clicked for one another (#134):
    one manages the inbound controller, the other the outbound bridge, and
    they are separately versioned and separately installed packages.
-->
<MenuItems>
    <!-- 1 · Matter devices Indigo controls -->
    <MenuItem id="commissionDeviceManually">
        <Name>Commission device by setup code (advanced)…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuCommissionDeviceManually</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="setupCode" type="textfield">
                <Label>Setup code (MT:... or 11 digits):</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="suggestedName" type="textfield">
                <Label>Name:</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="folder" type="menu">
                <Label>Folder:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getDeviceFolders" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <Description>Where the new Indigo device(s) land. Leave as "(no folder)" for the device-list root.</Description>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <!--
        Share a Matter device with another ecosystem… (issue #210). Placed
        right after the other additive action (commission) and before the
        destructive one (decommission stays last in this section per #134).

        Same "codes go to the event log" reasoning as Pair Matter Bridge…
        below (Indigo dialogs have no dynamic labels) — this is the reverse
        direction: the device is already commissioned to Indigo, and this
        opens a window so a SECOND ecosystem can add it too.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="shareMatterNode">
        <Name>Share a Matter device with another ecosystem…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuShareMatterNode</CallbackMethod>
        <ButtonTitle>Open sharing window</ButtonTitle>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="shareIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Opens a commissioning window on a Matter device Indigo already controls, so another ecosystem (Apple Home, Alexa, Google, Home Assistant) can add it too — without removing it from Indigo.

The pairing code is written to the Indigo EVENT LOG when you click below — Indigo dialogs cannot display a code that is generated as you press the button. Add the device in the other ecosystem's app as you would any Matter accessory.

A Matter pairing code is not durable: once used, or once the window expires (up to 15 minutes), that code stops working and there is no way to read it again — you would need to run this again for a fresh one.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="node" type="menu">
                <Label>Device (Matter node):</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getShareableNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
            </Field>
            <Field id="duration" type="textfield" defaultValue="900">
                <Label>Window stays open for (seconds):</Label>
                <Description>Matter allows 180 to 900 (3 to 15 minutes). 900 is the maximum and the default.</Description>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="decommissionDevice">
        <Name>Decommission Matter device…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuDecommissionDevice</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Removes the device from Indigo's Matter fabric and deletes its Indigo device(s). The device stays in Apple Home and any other ecosystem. Note: deleting the Indigo device alone does NOT do this — the plugin recreates it when it next reconciles with matter-server.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="node" type="menu">
                <Label>Device (Matter node):</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getMatterNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this removes the device from Indigo (other ecosystems are unaffected).</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <!--
        Replace a Matter node… (issue #364). Wording follows Indigo's own
        "Replacing a Z-Wave Device" flow.

        The preview is a read-only LIST, not a label, on purpose (D4): ConfigUI
        has no per-field change callback, so a label cannot update as the pickers
        change. A dynamicReload list can, and both pickers' CallbackMethod
        (replaceNodeChanged, a no-op) exists only to make Indigo re-run it.
        Same mechanism as exploreAttributes and exportList.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="replaceMatterNode">
        <Name>Replace a Matter node…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuReplaceNode</CallbackMethod>
        <ButtonTitle>Replace</ButtonTitle>
        <ConfigUI>
            <!-- Layout copies Manage Matter Exports: SHORT labels, the
                 explanation in <Description>. Indigo sizes the label column
                 to the longest Label, so one sentence-long checkbox label
                 squeezes every control (the preview list included) into a
                 narrow strip on the right. -->
            <Field id="replaceIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Points your existing Indigo devices at a re-paired device's new Matter node, then deletes the duplicate devices that node created.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replacePickHeading" type="label" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>1 · Pick the nodes</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replaceOld" type="menu">
                <Label>Keep devices of:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getReplaceOldNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>replaceNodeChanged</CallbackMethod>
                <Description>The old node, whose Indigo devices your automations use.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replaceNew" type="menu">
                <Label>Sync using node:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getReplaceNewNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>replaceNodeChanged</CallbackMethod>
                <Description>The re-paired device's new node. Matching nodes are listed first.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replaceCheckHeading" type="label" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>2 · Check and confirm</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replacePreview" type="list" rows="4" readonly="true">
                <Label>Preview:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getReplacePreview" dynamicReload="true"/>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replacePreviewNote" type="label" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>The reference check cannot see references inside plugin-action settings (for example Lamplighter zones).</Label>
            </Field>
            <!-- Set by replaceNodeChanged; visibility can only bind to a field value. Cosmetic:
                 menuReplaceNode re-plans and never reads it. -->
            <Field id="replaceShapeDiffers" type="textfield" hidden="true" defaultValue="false"/>
            <Field id="replaceAnyway" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false"
                   visibleBindingId="replaceShapeDiffers" visibleBindingValue="true"
                   alwaysUseInDialogHeightCalc="true">
                <Label>Matching only:</Label>
                <Description>The new node has different devices. Tick to move only the ones that match.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="replaceDecommissionOld" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>Remove old node:</Label>
                <Description>Also remove the old node from Indigo's Matter fabric (harmless if it no longer answers).</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>Confirm:</Label>
                <Description>The duplicate devices on the new node will be deleted.</Description>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>

    <!--
        Read-only diagnostics (issue #191; Report Thread mesh… added by #334).
        Deliberately in section 1, beside the devices they describe, rather
        than with the plumbing in sections 3-5: these answer questions about a
        DEVICE.

        All three report into the EVENT LOG, and that is forced rather than
        chosen. Indigo's ConfigUI has no per-field change callback — the only
        dynamic mechanism is <List dynamicReload="true">, which regenerates a
        list's OPTIONS — so a menu dialog physically cannot display an answer.
        Same constraint and same resolution as the pairing codes in section 3.

        NONE OF THEM WRITES ANYTHING, and that is settled (2026-08-11), not an
        omission awaiting a follow-up: a generic attribute writer is
        unvalidated by construction, is device-global on devices joined to
        several ecosystems, and adds nothing over declaring another
        DeviceSetting — which is bounds-checked and verified by read-back.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="reportSettableSettings">
        <Name>Report settable Matter settings…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuReportSettings</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="reportIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Lists, in the Event Log, the Matter settings a device supports that this plugin does not offer yet. Nothing is changed and nothing is wrong with a device that reports some — it is a list of settings that could be added. Paste the result into a GitHub issue to ask for them.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="node" type="menu">
                <Label>Device:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getSurveyNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <Description>Devices report this automatically the first time they are seen; use this to ask again, or for devices added before this feature existed.</Description>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="exploreAttributes">
        <Name>Explore Matter attributes (advanced)…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuExploreAttributes</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="exploreIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Dumps what a Matter device reports, to the Event Log. Read-only — nothing is ever written to the device. Leave the attribute box empty for a full dump of the chosen scope; fill it in to read one attribute live from the device instead.</Label>
            </Field>
            <!--
                The node menu's CallbackMethod does nothing on purpose. It is
                the ONLY way to make Indigo round-trip to the plugin, which is
                what re-runs the two dynamicReload lists below — ConfigUI has
                no per-field change event, so a menu callback is the whole
                cascade mechanism.
            -->
            <Field id="node" type="menu">
                <Label>Device:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getExploreNodes" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>exploreNodeChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="endpoint" type="menu" defaultValue="all">
                <Label>Endpoint:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getExploreEndpoints" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>exploreNodeChanged</CallbackMethod>
                <Description>A device with several functions (or a bridge) has one endpoint per function. Endpoint 0 is the device itself.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="cluster" type="menu" defaultValue="all">
                <Label>Cluster:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getExploreClusters" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <Description>Only clusters this device actually reports are listed.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="attribute" type="textfield">
                <Label>Read one attribute live (optional):</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="attributeHelp" type="label" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>Leave empty for a full dump from the cached snapshot — complete and instant, but an unsubscribed value can be stale. To confirm one value against the device itself, choose an exact endpoint and cluster and enter an attribute id here (e.g. 0x4003). A battery or Thread device can take several seconds to answer.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="reportThreadMesh">
        <Name>Report Thread mesh…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuReportThreadMesh</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="threadMeshIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Prints the Thread mesh to the Event Log: roles, links and health flags.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="threadMeshReadOnly" type="label">
                <Label>Read-only — nothing is ever written to any device.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="liveReadSleepy" type="checkbox" defaultValue="true">
                <Label>Live-read sleepy devices first:</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="liveReadSleepyHelp" type="label" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>Slower but accurate — the cache can be hours stale for battery devices.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="threadMeshFooter" type="label">
                <Label>The report ends with a link to the visual mesh map page.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="threadMeshBackground" type="label">
                <Label>This dialog closes right away; the report follows in the Event Log.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>

    <MenuItem id="sepDevicesFromExport"/>

    <!--
        2 · Matter devices Indigo publishes (export).

        Manage Matter Exports (PRD-indigo-matter-export §5.1, UI-D)
        deliberately has NO <CallbackMethod>: with one, the dialog gets
        Execute/Cancel and every action has to funnel through Execute. Without
        one it gets a single Close button and the buttons below do the work —
        which is what the SDK recommends for a multi-action dialog, and what a
        master-detail pane needs.

        Shape is dictated by what Indigo's dialogs can actually do: a multi-
        select `list` has no CallbackMethod (no selection events) and a
        `textfield` has none either, so the master-detail is a filter textfield
        + a reload button + a SINGLE-select `menu` picker (menus DO have
        CallbackMethod) whose callback loads the picked device's saved settings
        into the sibling fields. Labels are static in Indigo, so runtime
        feedback goes in the read-only `exportStatus` textfield.
    -->
    <!--
        Pairing and fabric management (PRD-indigo-matter-export §6, E6).

        "Pair" is deliberately "open a pairing window", not "show the code": a
        Matter passcode is not durable, so once the first ecosystem commissions
        there IS no standing code, and every further admin needs a freshly
        derived one (BRIDGE_PROTOCOL §3.8).

        The codes go to the EVENT LOG, not into this dialog. Indigo dialogs have
        no dynamic labels and no image fields, so a value computed by the
        callback cannot be shown in the dialog that produced it. The QR lives on
        an IWS-served page whose URL is logged beside the codes.
    -->
    <!--
        Pair a robot vacuum (ADR-0019, BRIDGE_PROTOCOL §3.12). A vacuum is its
        own Matter node with its own code, so it needs its own pairing menu;
        same event-log channel and the same three cases as Pair Matter Bridge.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="pairMatterVacuum">
        <Name>Pair Matter Vacuum…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuPairMatterVacuum</CallbackMethod>
        <ButtonTitle>Show pairing code</ButtonTitle>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="bridgeTarget" type="menu">
                <Label>Bridge:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getBridgeTargets" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>bridgeTargetChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="vacuumIntro" type="label">
                <Label>A robot vacuum is exported as its own Matter accessory, not as part of the bridge, so it has its own pairing code. The code (and a link to a QR page) is written to the Indigo EVENT LOG.

If the vacuum has never been paired, its original code is shown. Once paired, a new window is opened for each further ecosystem.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="vacuumDevice" type="menu" defaultValue="0">
                <Label>Vacuum:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getStandaloneExports" dynamicReload="true"/>
            </Field>
            <Field id="duration" type="textfield" defaultValue="900">
                <Label>Window stays open for (seconds):</Label>
                <Description>Matter allows 180 to 900 (3 to 15 minutes).</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="vacuumReset" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>Unpair everywhere first:</Label>
                <Description>Removes the vacuum from EVERY ecosystem it is paired with and gives it new codes. Use this if it was paired somewhere you no longer control.</Description>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>


    <MenuItem id="sepExportFromServer"/>

    <!-- 3 · The Matter controller (matter-server) — its own plumbing -->
    <MenuItem id="installMatterServer">
        <Name>Install/update the Matter controller (matter-server)</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuInstallMatterServer</CallbackMethod>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="restartMatterServer">
        <Name>Restart the Matter controller</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuRestartMatterServer</CallbackMethod>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="reinstallMatterServerClean">
        <Name>Reinstall the Matter controller (clean)…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuReinstallMatterServerClean</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Deletes the installed Matter controller (matter-server) package (~/indigo-matter/node_modules) and reinstalls it fresh, then restarts. Your commissioned devices are NOT affected — the fabric/storage is left untouched. Use this if matter-server won't start after an upgrade (e.g. a wedged install or a "Storage is locked" error).</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this reinstalls matter-server (devices/pairings are kept).</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="showMatterServerLogs">
        <Name>Open the Matter controller log…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuShowMatterServerLogs</CallbackMethod>
    </MenuItem>

    <MenuItem id="sepServerFromBridge"/>

    <!--
        4 · The Matter bridge node's own plumbing.

        Its install item lives in its own section, one separator below
        matter-server's (#134): the two are separately versioned, separately
        installed npm packages, and clicking the wrong one bounces a working
        half of the plugin for nothing.

        The bridge's two recovery exits: the controller has had a clean
        reinstall and a restart since 2026.7; the bridge shipped with neither,
        which left a wedged install with a menu that reinstalls OVER the wedge,
        and a running node with no UI at all once the plugin is disabled.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="installBridgeNode">
        <Name>Install/update the Matter bridge</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuInstallBridgeNode</CallbackMethod>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="reinstallBridgeNodeClean">
        <Name>Reinstall the Matter bridge (clean)…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuReinstallBridgeNodeClean</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Removes the installed indigo-matter-bridge package from ~/indigo-matter and installs it fresh. Your ecosystem pairings and the exported accessories' identities are NOT affected — they live in the bridge's storage folder, which this never touches, and neither is matter-server.

Use this if the Matter bridge will not start after an upgrade and a plain "Install/update" has not fixed it.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this reinstalls the bridge (pairings are kept).</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="stopBridgeNode">
        <Name>Stop the Matter bridge…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuStopBridgeNode</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="bridgeTarget" type="menu">
                <Label>Bridge:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getBridgeTargets" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>bridgeTargetChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Stops the Matter bridge process and removes its LaunchAgent, so it does not come back when this Mac restarts.

Your ecosystem pairings and your export list are NOT changed. Exported accessories will show as unavailable in Apple Home, Alexa and Google until the bridge runs again — which it does by itself the next time you add or change an export.

You normally never need this: the bridge starts and stops with your export list. It is here for the case where you want the process gone now — before disabling the plugin, for instance, which otherwise leaves it running with nothing to control it.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand exported accessories go unavailable until the bridge runs again.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>

    <MenuItem id="sepBridgeFromRecovery"/>

    <!--
        5 · Backup and recovery — rare, and the last two are destructive.

        "Back up the Matter fabric…" was "Export fabric backup…", where
        "Export" meant save-to-disk in a menu where it means the outbound
        bridge everywhere else. The callback names are unchanged.

        The two export-side recovery exits (BRIDGE_PROTOCOL §3.10/§3.11) sit
        at the bottom. Both existed only as node commands until E6:
        `rebuild_endpoint_map` had NO caller anywhere in the plugin, while
        three user-facing strings (the attach-refusal error, the client's
        remedy text and the export dialog's status line) told the user to
        "confirm the rebuild in the plugin". There was nothing to confirm —
        the only way out of the refuse-to-start state was to hand-edit a JSON
        file in the storage dir.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="exportFabricBackup">
        <Name>Back up the Matter fabric…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuExportFabricBackup</CallbackMethod>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="restoreFabricBackup">
        <Name>Restore a fabric backup…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuRestoreFabricBackup</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Restoring REPLACES the current fabric and restarts matter-server. If the backup includes the Matter bridge node's storage, that is restored too — if the bridge node is running it is stopped and started around the swap; a stopped bridge stays stopped. The current fabric (and bridge storage, if restored) is moved aside, not deleted, so a bad restore is reversible. Requires 'Manage LaunchAgent' to be on.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="backup" type="menu">
                <Label>Backup to restore:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getFabricBackups"/>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this replaces the current fabric (and the Matter bridge node's storage, if the backup has it) and will restart matter-server.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <MenuItem id="rebuildEndpointMap">
        <Name>Rebuild Matter Endpoint Map…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuRebuildEndpointMap</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="bridgeTarget" type="menu">
                <Label>Bridge:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getBridgeTargets" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>bridgeTargetChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="warning" type="label">
                <Label>Use this ONLY when the plugin says the bridge node is refusing to export because its endpoint-number map is unreadable. The map is the bridge's safety record of which Matter accessory number belongs to which Indigo device; rebuilding it discards the unreadable record and adopts whatever numbers exist now as the new one.

Rebuilding renumbers NOTHING, so it cannot itself duplicate accessories. What you see afterwards depends on which fault brought you here:

- If only the map file was damaged, the accessories keep their numbers, no paired ecosystem (Apple Home, Alexa, Google) sees any further change, and the rebuild simply clears the refusal.

- If the bridge's Matter storage was lost, your ecosystems are ALREADY holding accessories under the old numbers — those are dead, you have to delete them by hand, and any names, rooms, scenes and automations built on them are lost. That damage was done by the storage loss; rebuilding accepts it rather than causing it.

The unreadable file is kept beside the new one as endpoint-map.json.corrupt-&lt;timestamp&gt;; if you have a backup of the original, restoring that is better than rebuilding. No pairing is touched, and no Indigo device is changed.

If the bridge node is NOT refusing, this does nothing useful and is refused: there is no fault to recover from, and a rebuild would discard the retained endpoint numbers of every device you are not currently exporting — the ones that make re-adding a device restore the same accessory rather than create a second one.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="confirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this replaces the endpoint-number record and cannot be undone.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <!--
        Migrate an exported accessory… (issue #246). Moves a LIVE exported
        accessory's identity to a different Indigo device — replaced
        hardware, a module moved to new gear, a device rebuilt under a new
        id, with the OLD Indigo device still present.

        This is the LIVE-source path, and since issue #274 it is the ONLY
        path: a deleted Indigo device destroys its accessory, so an identity
        left behind is no longer recoverable and the re-adopt fallback that
        used to sit below this entry is gone. Migrate BEFORE deleting the
        old device. The identity never lapses, so no paired ecosystem ever
        processes a removal, and room, name, scenes and automations survive
        BY CONSTRUCTION — the 2026-08-18 jarvis measurement behind ADR-0011.

        Declarative under the hood (ADR-0011): the store retargets the entry
        in one atomic `replace_all`, and a full mid-session `attach`
        (`ExportBridge.reattach()`) carries it to the bridge node, which
        reconciles it as an in-place REKEY — same `Endpoint.id`, same
        accessory number.

        Master-detail shape: `migrateSource` gets a CallbackMethod
        (`migrateSourceChanged`) purely to make Indigo round-trip and re-run
        `migrateDevice`'s own dynamicReload list, filtered to devices that
        can take the picked source's role.
    -->
    <MenuItem id="migrateExport">
        <Name>Migrate an exported accessory…</Name>
        <CallbackMethod>menuMigrateExport</CallbackMethod>
        <ConfigUI>
            <Field id="bridgeTarget" type="menu">
                <Label>Bridge:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getBridgeTargets" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>bridgeTargetChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateIntro" type="label">
                <Label>Moves a live exported accessory to a different Indigo device — for replaced hardware, a module moved to new gear, or a device rebuilt under a new id. Nothing is removed and nothing is re-paired: Apple Home and every other paired ecosystem keep the accessory's room, name, scenes and automations, because from their side nothing happened. The old device stays in Indigo, no longer exported.

A ROLE CHANGE is not what this is for — export the replacement device normally instead, and it becomes a new accessory. A device that cannot take the source accessory's role is refused below.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateSource" type="menu">
                <Label>Currently exported as:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getMigrateSources" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <CallbackMethod>migrateSourceChanged</CallbackMethod>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateSourceHelp" type="label" fontColor="darkgray">
                <Label>Every accessory this plugin is currently exporting.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateDevice" type="menu">
                <Label>Move it to:</Label>
                <List class="self" method="getMigrateDevices" dynamicReload="true"/>
                <Description>Only devices that can take the accessory's role are offered. A ● marks a device that is already exported.</Description>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateWarning" type="label">
                <Label>If the device you pick above already has its own accessory (marked ●), migrating removes THAT accessory from every ecosystem and puts the migrated one in its place instead.</Label>
            </Field>
            <Field id="migrateConfirm" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
                <Label>I understand this moves the accessory to a different Indigo device.</Label>
            </Field>
        </ConfigUI>
    </MenuItem>
    <!-- issue #204 / ADR-0008: the ONLY way to bring back a matterNode device
         a user deleted by hand — device_sync deliberately does not recreate
         one it has been told to leave deleted (unlike every endpoint device,
         which self-heals on the next reconcile). No ConfigUI: this clears
